,title,link,text,language,family
0,Tunica language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tunica_language,"The Tunica or Luhchi Yoroni (or Tonica, or less common form Yuron) [2] language is a language isolate that was spoken in the Central and Lower Mississippi Valley in the United States by Native American Tunica peoples.",Tunica,Language isolate
1,Tunica Language | Language and Linguistics | Research ... - EBSCO,https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/language-and-linguistics/tunica-language,"The Tunica language, historically spoken by the Tunica people in areas along the lower Yazoo River and the eastern shore of the Mississippi River in Louisiana, is classified as a language isolate but has been controversially associated with the Gulf Group of the Penutian family.",Tunica,Language isolate
2,the Tunica Language,https://tunica.wp.tulane.edu/about/about-the-tunica-language/,"His use of Tunica in everyday life declined abruptly when his mother, a more fluent speaker, died in 1915. Tunica is what is known as a “language isolate”, meaning no known linguistic relatives of the language exist.",Tunica,Language isolate
3,Tunica language - grokipedia.com,https://grokipedia.com/page/Tunica_language,"The Tunica language is classified as a language isolate, lacking any demonstrable genetic affiliation with other language families despite its historical proximity to Muskogean, Siouan, and Caddoan languages in the Lower Mississippi Valley.",Tunica,Language isolate
